In the field of laser spectroscopy, various methods have been explored to shield a qubit-clock transition from external influences. Among these, hyper-Ramsey (HR) spectroscopy denoted as HR3$_{\pi}$, was proposed in 2010~\cite{Yudin:2010} and demonstrated experimentally in 2012~\cite{Huntemann:2012}. This method drastically reduces AC-Stark shifts induced by laser coupling to off-resonant atomic states and neighboring Zeeman sub-levels attributed to a small elliptic polarization of the probe~\cite{Huntemann:2012,Yudin:2023}.
A modified version of HR spectroscopy was proposed and demonstrated in 2016 within optical lattices probing ultra-narrow clock transitions of bosons eliminating probe-field-induced AC-Stark shifts by three orders of magnitude even with significant errors in shift compensation~\cite{Hobson:2016}. Single-ion optical clocks based on $^{171}$Yb$^{+}$~\cite{Huntemann:2016}, $^{40}$Ca$^{+}$~\cite{Huang:2022} and $^{176}$Lu$^{+}$~\cite{Zhiqiang:2023} have exploited HR spectroscopy reporting an impressive low uncertainty budget at the $10^{-18}$ relative level of accuracy.
However, the asymmetric temporal position of the intermediate refocusing pulse in HR spectroscopy is required for the clock interferometer to be sensitive to the
detuning of the probe beam with the highest signal contrast. It is opposed to the Mach-Zehnder atomic interferometer which eliminates not only inhomogeneous broadening effects but sensitivity to frequency offsets~\cite{Gillot:2016}. Consequently, the original HR scheme still exhibits a fundamental technical drawback suffering from residual probe frequency offsets and pulse errors associated to weak decoherence~\cite{Tabatchikova:2013} requiring a combination of several sequences of phase-shifted composite pulses to eliminate some of these distortions~\cite{Ramsey:1951,Zanon-Willette:2017,Zanon-Willette:2018}. Robustness of HR spectroscopy and hybrid schemes were finally investigated in presence of trapped-ion heating processes~\cite{Kuznetsov:2019} and in an optically dense medium~\cite{Barantsev:2020}.
