\indent In this letter, we present a significant improvement of HR spectroscopy by inserting a single phase-shifted refocusing pulse to generate dynamical decoupling through symmetry considerations~\cite{Zanardi:1999,Shaka:1987,Levitt:2008}.
The symmetrization of the temporal position of a single refocusing pulse between Ramsey pulses generates a dynamical-decoupling effect which suppresses environmental low frequency noise impact and decoherence-induced frequency-shifts on interferences. Our new interrogation scheme denoted as dynamically-decoupled HR spectroscopy (DDHR3$_{\pi}$), presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:DD-HR-protocols}, is merging composite pulse laser spectroscopy~\cite{Yudin:2010,Zanon-Willette:2018,Zanon-Willette:202-1,Zanon-Willette:2019,Zanon-Willette:2022} with spin-echoes~\cite{Hahn:1950,Carr:1954} to preserve robustness against probe-induced light-shifts and frequency offsets and drifts. We introduce an intermediate rotary Hahn-echo pulse~\cite{Solomon:1959}, coupled to a negative detuning~\cite{Vitanov:2015,Sadzak:2022,Ivanov:2022,Kyoseva:2019}, which  suppresses low noise fluctuation and frequency offsets that alter the optical qubit excitation probability by approximately the same amount in both free evolution zones. At the same time, residual clock frequency-shifts related to decoherence and laser-probe-intensity fluctuations that can not be compensated by the original HR protocol~\cite{Tabatchikova:2013,Beloy:2018} are suppressed. Our DDHR scheme is then extended to multiple refocusing pulses restoring sensitivity of quantum interferences to the laser probe frequency detuning at the output of the interferometer as demonstrated in refs~\cite{Chaneliere:2015,McCormick:2019}.
