The original hyper-Ramsey HR3$_{\pi}$ protocol is implemented by using a $90^{\circ}_{+}-180^{\circ}_{+,\pi}90^{\circ}_{+}$ composite pulse sequence without sign modification of the laser probe frequency detuning as reported in Fig.~\ref{fig:DD-HR-protocols}(a)-(b). Our new DDHR3$_{\pi}$ protocol is realized by inserting an intermediate refocusing pulse with a negative laser probe detuning (i.e the laser probe is red-detuned from the perturbed qubit-clock resonance) applied midway between Ramsey pulses as $90^{\circ}_{+}-180^{\circ}_{-,\pi}-90^{\circ}_{+}$. Composite pulses and associated laser probe parameters including distortions are reported in Fig.~\ref{fig:DD-HR-protocols}(a)-(c).
Swapping the probe detuning sign is realized by the intermediate $180^{\circ}_{-,\pi}$ time-reversal pulse action on the quantum system evolution.
The laser probe frequency detuning can suffer from uncompensated residual light-shifts $\Delta_{LS}$ associated to potential static frequency offsets or residual frequency drifts $\varepsilon_{P}$ due to technical defects as described in Fig.~\ref{fig:DD-HR-protocols}. The residual light-shift is induced by excitation pulses while a technical defect as a frequency offset or drift is always present over the entire sequence of pulses.
The propagator matrix of the $l$-th pulse $\left[\widetilde{\vartheta}_{l}\right]_{\pm}$ (l$=1,2,3$) driving SU(2) spinor dynamics reads~\cite{Zanon-Willette:2019,Zanon-Willette:2022}
\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
	\left[\widetilde{\vartheta}_{l}\right]_{\pm}\equiv
		\left( \begin{array}{cc}
			\cos\widetilde{\vartheta}_{l}e^{i\phi_{l}} & -i e^{-i\varphi_{l}}\sin\widetilde{\vartheta}_{l} \\
			-i e^{i\varphi_{l}}\sin\widetilde{\vartheta}_{l} & \cos\widetilde{\vartheta}_{l}e^{-i\phi_{l}}\\
		\end{array}\right),
	\end{split}
	\label{eq:Cayley-Klein-Matrix-1}
\end{equation}
with a pulse phase $\varphi_{l}$ related to the Rabi frequency $\Omega_{l}$ and where $\pm$ means we can apply a positive or a negative effective probe detuning $\widetilde{\delta}_{l}\rightarrow\pm\widetilde{\delta}_{l}+\varepsilon_{P}$ where $\widetilde{\delta}_{l}=\delta-\Delta_{LS}$.
Cayley-Klein phase angles are introduced as:
\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
\widetilde{\vartheta}_{l}=\arcsin\left[\frac{\Omega_{l}}{\omega_{l}}\sin\widetilde{\theta}_{l}\right], \hspace{0.25cm}\phi_{l}=\arctan\left[\frac{\widetilde{\delta}_{l}}{\omega_{l}}\tan\widetilde{\theta}_{l}\right].
	\end{split}
	\label{phase-angles}
\end{equation}
The pulse area is $\widetilde{\theta}_{l}=\omega_{l}\tau_{l}/2$ with a generalized Rabi frequency denoted as $\omega_{l}=\sqrt{\widetilde{\delta}_{l}^{2}+\Omega_{l}^{2}}$.
The free evolution propagator $\left[\pm\right]$ while switching laser fields is
\begin{equation}
	\begin{split}
	\left[\pm\right]\equiv
		\left(\begin{array}{cc}
			e^{i(\pm\delta+\varepsilon_{P})T/2} & 0 \\
			0 & e^{-i(\pm\delta+\varepsilon_{P})T/2}\\
		\end{array}\right).
	\end{split}
	\label{eq:Cayley-Klein-Matrix-2}
\end{equation}
Transition probabilities associated to our protocols are computed by multiplying propagator matrices leading to:
\begin{align}
    \textup{HR3}_{\varphi}&\equiv\left|\left\langle e\left|\left[90^{\circ}\right]_{+}\left[180^{\circ}_{\varphi}\right]_{+}\left[+\right]\left[90^{\circ}\right]_{+}\right|g\right\rangle\right|^{2}\label{HR-probability},\\
\textup{DDHR3}_{\varphi}&\equiv\left|\left\langle e\left|\left[90^{\circ}\right]_{+}\left[-\right]\left[180^{\circ}_{\varphi}\right]_{-}\left[+\right]\left[90^{\circ}\right]_{+}\right|g\right\rangle\right|^{2}\label{DDHR-probability}.
\end{align}
including a phase-shifted refocusing pulse with $\varphi\in[0,\pi]$. Exact analytic solutions of transition probabilities can be found in~\cite{Zanon-Willette:2022}.
We plot HR3$_{\pi}$ and DDHR3$_{\pi}$ interference fringes versus the probe frequency detuning in Fig.~\ref{fig:interferences}(a)-(b) and interference contrast of HR3$_{\pi}$ and DDHR3$_{\pi}$ signals in Fig.~\ref{fig:interferences-contrast} based on Eq.~(\ref{HR-probability}) and Eq.~(\ref{DDHR-probability}). The DDHR3$_{\pi}$ protocol is robust to a large $50~\%$ pulse area variation while the HR3$_{\pi}$ scheme has already lost half of its population fraction. This is the first evidence of a robust sequence of composite pulses realized by a temporal symmetrization of laser excitation pulses on the optical qubit-clock transition.

\indent We plot in Fig.~\ref{fig:central-fringe-LS-EPS} the clock frequency-shift as a function of a residual probe-induced light-shift $\Delta_{LS}$ including or not a small correction by a decoherence term $\gamma_{c}$. In Fig.~\ref{fig:central-fringe-LS-EPS}, the HR3$_{\pi}$ frequency-shift of the central fringe exhibits a nonlinear cubic dependence with the probe-induced light-shift~\cite{Yudin:2010,Huntemann:2012} while the DDHR3$_{\pi}$ frequency-shift is immune. This is the second evidence of a robust sequence of composite pulses by inserting a modulation of the laser probe frequency detunings with opposite signs into a sequence of composite pulses~\cite{Kyoseva:2019,Ivanov:2022}.
