Also, we plot the central fringe frequency-shift with respect to residual probe-induced light-shift using Optical-Bloch equations including a decoherence term describing a finite laser probe linewidth~\cite{Tabatchikova:2013,Zanon-Willette:2017}. We report our results in Fig.~\ref{fig:central-fringe-LS-EPS} demonstrating that decoherence does not affect the reliability of the DDHR3$_{\pi}$ protocol in the presence of residual light-shifts, while the HR3$_{\pi}$ protocol breaks down in the full suppression of probe-induced light-shifts~\cite{Tabatchikova:2013}.

\indent Then, we plot the interference contrast and sensitivity of HR3$_{\pi}$ and DD-HR3$_{\pi}$ protocols to a small frequency offset or drift $\varepsilon_{P}$ related to technical defects of the laser probe or external distortions during the interrogation process. In Fig.~\ref{fig:Central-fringe-shift-EPS}(a), the HR3$_{\pi}$ protocol exhibits a steepest linear slope of the frequency-shift versus a residual probe detuning offset. The DDHR3$_{\pi}$ protocol unveils a non linear sensitivity to probe frequency detuning offsets even in presence of variation of pulse areas by several percents.
Furthermore, robust dispersive DDHR error signals (DDDHR3$_{\pm\varphi}$$=\textup{DDHR3}_{+\varphi}-\textup{DDHR3}_{-\varphi}$) can be produced by substracting two or more phase-shifted transition probabilities~\cite{Ramsey:1951,Zanon-Willette:2017,Zanon-Willette:2018,Zanon-Willette:2022}.
A DDDHR3$_{\pm\pi/4}$ error signal is reported in Fig.~\ref{fig:Central-fringe-shift-EPS}(a) removing the residual nonlinear dependence with the probe-induced frequency offset. A drastic improvement of interference contrast by the DDHR3$_{\pi}$ scheme versus the free evolution time T is presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:Central-fringe-shift-EPS}(b).
