\indent We now turn our attention to the influence of probe-laser-intensity fluctuations on HR3$_{\pi}$ and DDHR3$_{\pi}$ spectroscopy. Following a model from~\cite{Beloy:2018}, we have investigated the effect of the fluctuation of the Rabi probe field coupled to probe-induced light-shifts on these composite pulse protocols and plotted the results in Fig.~\ref{fig:HR-DDHR-field-fluctuation}.
The DDHR3$_{\pi}$ protocol is still immune with an error-free clock operation (solid red stars) while the HR3$_{\pi}$ protocol (blue solid dots) is compromised with a clock frequency-shift acquiring a lower-order quartic
dependence with Rabi field fluctuations. This is another demonstration of fault-tolerance of our DDHR composite pulse protocol.

\indent We finally propose to extend noise resilient DDHR spectroscopy to sequences with multiple refocusing pulses inspired by NMR techniques from periodic driving control~\cite{Vandersypen:2005,Jones:2024} to dynamical-decoupling~\cite{Meiboom:1958,Hennig:2001,Khodjasteh:2005,Souza:2012,Uhrig:2007,Lee:2008,Yang:2008,Yang:2011}.
Dynamical decoupling methods aim not only to suppress dephasing errors from the environment but also to eliminate undesired quasi-static or time-dependent interactions by employing control sequences of multiple Hahn-echo refocusing pulses on atomic systems like qubits~\cite{Gordon:2008,Suter:2016,Szwer:2011,Yu:2013,Chow:2021} and matter-waves~\cite{Berg:2015}. Usually, a single Hahn-echo is sufficient in the case of quasi-static interactions (see Fig.~\ref{fig:Central-fringe-shift-EPS}(b)). However, low noise fluctuation can cause external interactions to vary~\cite{Kabytayev:2014}. Consequently, it is imperative to apply rotary Hahn-echoes repeatedly, preferably at a rate that is fast in comparison to the fluctuation time scale.\\
To simulate dynamical decoupling sequences of pulses in presence of low noise perturbation, we establish first a set of expansion rules for symmetrization of the qubit-clock dynamics driven by phase-shifted Hahn refocusing pulses toggling signs of frequency-shifted probe detunings as following:\\
- An odd number of phase-shifted Hahn-refocusing pulses encapsulated by free evolution zones with opposite sign can be inserted into Eq.~\ref{DDHR-probability} as
   $\begin{array}{c}\left[\mp\right]\left[180^{\circ}_{\pi/0}\right]_{\mp}\left[\pm\right]\hspace{0.2cm}\end{array}$\\
- An equal number of positive and negative free evolution zones $\left[+\right],\left[-\right]$ symmetrically positioned over the entire sequence of pulses.\\
- An equal number of pulse areas $\left[\widetilde{\vartheta}_{l}\right]_{\pm}$ made of positive and negative probe frequency detunings.\\
Then, in our simulations, we introduce different noise sources as a large imperfection of the Rabi field coupling $\Omega\pm\delta\Omega$ and AC-Stark shift $\Delta_{LS}\pm\delta\Delta_{LS}$ while inserting a variable frequency offset $\varepsilon_{p}\pm\delta\varepsilon_{p}$. We treat these noise sources as quasi-static, where each noise is modeled by a random value from a Gaussian distribution with standard deviation.
Following our expansion rules, DDHR spectroscopy incorporate quantum control protocols related to conventional dynamical decoupling like periodic-DD (PDDHR), concatenated-DD (CDDHR) interrogation protocols and Uhrig-DD (UDDHR) time-optimized sequences of rotary Hahn-echo pulses. They effectively disconnect laser probe and decoherence inducing frequency-shifts of interferences in agreement with the results of Fig.~\ref{fig:central-fringe-LS-EPS}, Fig.~\ref{fig:Central-fringe-shift-EPS} and Fig.~\ref{fig:HR-DDHR-field-fluctuation}.
We also introduce quantum logic schemes synthesizing noise filters based on Walsh-Hadamard patterns~\cite{Ball:2016,Soare:2014}.
The generation of Walsh-Hadamard-Hahn $\textup{WAHAHA}^{\textup{j}}_{2^{k}}$ refocusing building-blocks (where $\textup{j}=\textup{s},\textup{h},\textup{p}$ indicates sequential, Hadamard and Paley matrix row ordering) is realized by a recursive application (initialization stage $\textup{k}=1$) such that:
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}
\textup{WAHAHA}^{\textup{j}}_{2^{k}}=\left[
                     \begin{array}{cc}
                       \textup{H}_{2^{k-1}} &  \textup{H}_{2^{k-1}} \\
                        \textup{H}_{2^{k-1}} & -\textup{H}_{2^{k-1}} \\
                     \end{array}
                   \right]^{\textup{j}}_{2^{k}},\textup{H}_{2}=\left[
                     \begin{array}{cc}
                       + & + \\
                       + & - \\
                     \end{array}
                   \right]
\end{split}
\label{eq:WAHAHA}
\end{equation}
Following Eq.~\ref{eq:WAHAHA}, we generate $\textup{WAHAHA}^{\textup{s}}_{2^{k}}$ sequences of pulses in a sequential order shown in the upper part of Fig.~\ref{fig:Table-PDD-UDD-contrast}. We have identified some pulse sequences of $\textup{WAHAHA}^{\textup{s}}_{2^{k}}$ (k$=3-5$) equivalent to PDDHR protocols including 3 and 7 rotary Hahn-echo pulses or to CDDHR sequences including respectively 5 and 21 pulses.
Particular pulse sequences of WAHAHA and CDD protocols are formally equivalent to aperiodic self-similar Thue-Morse (TM$_{2^{k}}$) sequences~\cite{Schat:2007}. The TM algorithm was applied for the first time to probe a Rabi clock resonance of a single trapped $^{171}$Yb$^{+}$ ion minimizing low frequency noise fluctuation of the optical reference cavity~\cite{Tofful:2024}.
The lower part of Fig.~\ref{fig:Table-PDD-UDD-contrast} shows interference contrast of HR3$_{\pi}$, PDDHR3$_{\pi}$ and UDDHR3$_{\pi}$ protocols versus an increasing number of refocusing pulses. Replacing the PDDHR3$_{\pi}$ scheme by a UDDHR3$_{\pi}$ sequence of N refocusing pulses within \textit{magic} time intervals following T$_{n}=\frac{T}{2}\left(\cos[(n-1)\pi/(N+1)]-\cos[n\pi/(N+1)]\right)$ (for $1\leq n\leq N+1$) produces the highest interference contrast against noise.\\
\indent DDHR spectroscopy is a fault-tolerant interrogation protocol that has been designed to outperform hyper-Ramsey robustness against residual probe-induced frequency-shifts in presence of probe intensity fluctuation, residual drifts and noisy electromagnetic trapping fields. Optimal quantum control methods may be used for error-robust DDHR spectroscopy tailored to specific noise sources~\cite{Khaneja:2005,Glaser:2015}. Near-optimal DDHR schemes may be explored by nesting DD sequences for contrast optimization~\cite{West:2010} while erasing residual technical pulse distortions by autobalanced DDHR spectroscopy~\cite{Zanon-Willette:2022}. Executing efficient spin squeezing in optical clocks may require multiple refocusing pulses and compensation of errors~\cite{Zhang:2016,Zhang:2021,Yang:2025,Pang:2025} that might be compatible with our DDHR protocols.
Optical tweezer clocks~\cite{Young:2020,Kaufman:2021}, Coulomb ion crystals~\cite{Keller:2019,Yu:2024,Hausser:2025}, multiple highly-charged ion clocks~\cite{Kozlov:2018,King:2022,Schmidt:2024}, $^{229}$Th3$^{+}$ single-ion nuclear clocks~\cite{Campbell:2012,Peik:2021} and neutral atom lattice clocks based on optical high-order multipolar transitions~\cite{Ishiyama:2024,Klusener:2024} should benefit from interrogation schemes preserving long-lived atomic interferences against inhomogeneities and broadening mechanisms. DDHR spectroscopy may finally enable robust quantum sensing and metrology with ultracold atoms and molecules offering higher sensitivity to track dark matter and test new physics beyond the Standard Model~\cite{Safronova:2018,DeMille:2024,Zaheer:2025}.\\
