\subsection{GIC maximum and standard deviation}
\label{sect:max_std}

In the previous section, it was shown that the timeseries of GIC were only weakly correlated between sites, with an average correlation between all pairs of $\sim 0.3$. In this section, we consider if the standard deviation during the storm, $\sigma$, and maximum GIC magnitude, $|\mbox{GIC}_{\text{max}}|$, and can be estimated using site geomagnetic latitude, $\lambda$, and $\beta$.

We construct regression models of the form $\sigma = a\lambda + c$ and $\sigma = a\beta + c$ and similar forms for $|\mbox{GIC}_{\text{max}}|$ to determine if geomagnetic latitude and $\beta$, can be used to estimate them.

(We used $\beta$ and $\log_{10}\beta$ in our regression models and found that one with $\beta$ had a correlation coefficient $\sim$ 0.1 greater than one with $\log_{10}\beta$ for both $\sigma$ and $|\mbox{GIC}_{\text{max}}|$. However, the difference in correlation coefficient is not statistically significant.)

In Figure~\ref{fig:scatter2}a and b, we see that both $\sigma$ and $|\mbox{GIC}_{\text{max}}|$ have a moderate linear relationship, with both models having $\mbox{cc}=0.54$. Visually, this relationship seems applicable for for $44$ of the $47$ sites because the sites with the four largest values of $\sigma$ and $|\mbox{GIC}_{\text{max}}|$ deviate significantly from the regression line. 

In Figure~\ref{fig:scatter2}c and d, we see that $\beta$ produces similar correlations.

To determine if $\lambda$ and $\beta$ have independent information that for predicting $\sigma$ and $|\mbox{GIC}_{\text{max}}|$, we compute the 2--D model

$\sigma = a\lambda + b\beta + c$

\noindent
the results of which are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:scatter2}e and f. The results of all models are given in tables~\ref{table:regression_std} and \ref{table:regression_max}. The difference in the AIC for the 2--D models with the AIC for the 1-D models indicates that the 2--D model is better than either of the 1--D models, statistically.

