\vspace{1cm}
\noindent\textbf{Abstract}:
 I proposed a unified model of particle physic and cosmology in \cite {1}, which can successfully account for many things of the inflation, dark energy, dark matter, neutrino mass and baryogenesis. Based on the model, I here focus on the fermion flavour issues, I introduce a realistic flavour symmetry to address the fermion mass and mixing, establish the relationship of the quark mixing and lepton mixing, and clarify the source of the difference between them. From the model Lagrangian I derive a neutrino mass matrix with a special structure form and fewer parameters, its numerical solutions are precisely fitting to all the data of the neutrino mass spectrum and lepton mixing, and clearly show a stable predicting power. In addition, I suggest a new scenario of the leptogenesis, which arises from two CP-asymmetric decays of a superheavy neutral Dirac fermion. This mechanism is closely related to the neutrino flavour violation, I derive its relationship with the neutrino mass and mixing. In short, the model can simply and elegantly address the fermion flavour issues and account for the matter-antimatter asymmetry, therefore it is very realistic and believable, we expect the ongoing and future experiments to test the model.

\vspace{1cm}
\noindent\textbf{Keywords}: beyond standard model; flavour symmetry; fermion mass and mixing; leptogenesis

\newpage
\noindent\textbf{I. Introduction}

\vspace{0.3cm}
 The standard model of particle physics (SM) have successfully accounted for numerous experimental measurements at the current day \cite{2}, nevertheless it contains too many fermions: six flavours of the quarks and leptons, respectively, further it has a plethora of the flavour parameters related with them, this mystery of the fermion flavours are far understood by us, and their implications for the cosmology are also unknown. At the present day, by means of plenty of analyses of the particle experimental data, the particle physicists have accurately measured the quark mixing matrix $U_{\text{CKM}}$ in the quark charged current couplings \cite{3}, and the lepton mixing matrix $U_{\text{PMNS}}$ in the lepton charged current couplings \cite{4}, and the two mass-squared differences in the neutrino oscillation \cite{5}, their current best-fit values are given as follows \cite{2},
\begin{alignat}{1}
 &U_{\text{CKM}}: \:\sin\theta_{12}\approx0.22501\,,\hspace{0.3cm} \sin\theta_{23}\approx0.04183\,,\hspace{0.3cm} 
    \sin\theta_{13}\approx0.003732\,,\hspace{0.3cm} \delta\approx0.3651\pi\,, \nonumber\\
 &U_{\text{PMNS}}: \:\sin\theta_{12}\approx0.554\,,\hspace{0.3cm} \sin\theta_{23}\approx0.745\,,\hspace{0.3cm} 
    \sin\theta_{13}\approx0.148\,,\hspace{0.3cm} \delta\approx1.19\,\pi\,, \nonumber\\
 & \triangle m^{2}_{21}\approx7.53\times10^{-5}\;\text{eV}^{2},\hspace{0.3cm}
    \triangle m^{2}_{32}\approx2.455\times10^{-3}(\text{NO})\,\text{or}\,-2.53\times10^{-3}(\text{IO})\;\text{eV}^{2},
\end{alignat}
 where $U_{\text{CKM}}$ and $U_{\text{PMNS}}$ are parameterized by the standard form, refer to the following Eq. (9), the abbreviations of NO and IO respectively denote the normal and inverted order of the neutrino mass spectrum. Why do these two mixing matrices appear in the gauge couplings of the quarks and leptons? What reason does the great difference between them arise from? Where does the CP-violation source of the lepton mixing stem from? What is really the true flavour symmetry of the fermions? or it is merely an imaginary symmetry? Why the neutrino masses are only $\sim0.1$ eV? which are far smaller than the charged lepton masses. What is really the mechanism by which the neutrino mass is generated? In addition, whether there is a connection between the fermion favours and the baryogenesis or leptogenesis? What roles the fermion favours play in the evolution of the early universe? All of these issues make up the puzzles about the fermion favour physics. They have therefore become the most challenging research in particle physics and cosmology. The flavour physics is currently attracting more and more attentions of theoretical and experimental physicists \cite{6}.

 At present, numerous theories have been proposed to address the above-mentioned issues about the fermion flavours. They include some extensions of the SM \cite{7}, some grand unified models \cite{8}, many special models of the fermion flavours \cite{9}, a great deal of neutrino mass models \cite{10}, a number of mechanisms of neutrino mass generation \cite{11}, and some mechanisms of the baryogenesis and leptogenesis \cite{12}, and so on. However, a wide variety of the proposals have a common shortcoming, namely they are only aiming at one or two specific aspects of the fermion flavour issues rather than considering internal connections among them, in particular, the organic unification of particle physics and cosmology. In fact, the uniqueness of the origin and evolution of the universe destines that these particle phenomena are by no means isolated, but rather closely related to the cosmological phenomena. In addition, some models of the fermion flavours are too illusory to realistic, the suggested flavour symmetries are even an imaginary and unmeasurable thing, these theories are obviously inadvisable. The theoretical and experimental investigations of particle physics have clearly indicated that a realistic flavour model should be testable, each of its parameters should be measurable rather than unphysical.

 I proposed a new extension of the SM in the reference \cite{1}, which covers both the SM sector and the dark sector beyond the SM, it can successfully account for many things of the inflation, dark energy, dark matter, neutrino mass and baryogenesis in a unified and integrated way. Here I specially discuss the fermion flavour problems in the model. My starting point is based on the principle that the fermion flavour symmetry should be simple, realistic and measurable, this is namely fitting to Occam's Razor. In fact, the most realistic flavour symmetry is exactly the global favour conservation of each flavour of the quarks and leptons. I carefully set the fermion flavour states and their corresponding flavour symmetries in the model, by which we can naturally and elegantly generate the fermion mass and mixing, in particular the neutrino mixing arises from the neutrino flavour violation, it is exactly the source of the difference between the quark mixing and the lepton mixing. From the model Lagrangian I derive the effective neutrino couplings and its mass matrix, and further I find out its numerical solutions by fitting the data of the neutrino mass spectrum and lepton mixing. In addition, I successfully implement a new leptogenesis scenario in the model, it is closely related to the neutrino mass and mixing, this mechanism can elegantly account for the observed baryon asymmetry. In short, this model is very realistic, reasonable and believable, therefore it is very worth studying in depth.

 The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. In Section II, I introduce the realistic flavour symmetry to generate the fermion mass and mixing, and then derive the neutrino mass matrix, and give its numerical solutions. In Section III, I discuss the new scenario of the leptogenesis. Section IV is devoted to conclusions.

\vspace{0.6cm}
\noindent\textbf{II. Fermion mass and Mixing}

\vspace{0.3cm}
 The particle model is based on the extension of the SM which was suggested by the author in \cite{1}, here I focus on the fermion flavour problem, discuss the origin of the fermion mass and mixing from a realistic flavour symmetry. I set the fermion flavour states and their corresponding global flavour symmetries as follows,
\begin{alignat}{1}
 &U(1)_{u}\otimes U(1)_{c}\otimes U(1)_{t}: \:q_{\alpha}=\left[\begin{matrix}u_{L\alpha}\\d'_{L\alpha}=U^{\text{CKM}}_{\alpha\beta}d_{L\beta}
   \end{matrix}\right],\hspace{0.3cm} u_{R\alpha}\,, \nonumber\\
 &U(1)_{d}\otimes U(1)_{s}\otimes U(1)_{b}: \:\tilde{q}_{\alpha}=U^{\dagger\text{CKM}}_{\alpha\beta}q_{\beta}=\left[\begin{matrix}
   u'_{L\alpha}=U^{\dagger\text{CKM}}_{\alpha\beta}u_{L\beta}\\d_{L\alpha}\end{matrix}\right],\hspace{0.3cm} d_{R\alpha}\,, \nonumber\\
 &U(1)_{\nu_{1}}\otimes U(1)_{\nu_{2}}\otimes U(1)_{\nu_{3}}: \:l_{\alpha}=\left[\begin{matrix}
   \nu_{L\alpha}\\e'_{L\alpha}=U^{\text{CKM}}_{\alpha\beta}e_{L\beta}\end{matrix}\right],\hspace{0.3cm} \nu_{R\alpha}\,, \nonumber\\
 &U(1)_{e}\otimes U(1)_{\mu}\otimes U(1)_{\tau}: \:\tilde{l}_{\alpha}=U^{\dagger\text{CKM}}_{\alpha\beta}l_{\beta}=\left[\begin{matrix}
   \nu'_{L\alpha}=U^{\dagger\text{CKM}}_{\alpha\beta}\nu_{L\beta}\\e_{L\alpha}\end{matrix}\right],\hspace{0.3cm} e_{R\alpha}\,, \nonumber\\
 &U(1)_{\nu_{1}+\nu_{2}+\nu_{3}}: \:N_{L}\,,\hspace{0.3cm} N_{R}\,,
\end{alignat}
 where all kinds of the notations are self-explanatory, their gauge quantum numbers and dark parities are all listed in Table 1 in \cite{1}. $[\alpha,\beta=1,2,3]$ are the generation (or family) indices, likewise, the subscripts of $[u,c,t]$, $[d,s,b]$, etc, are only employed to denote the meanings of the three generations, namely corresponding to $\alpha=1,2,3$, one should not misunderstand them. The left-handed doublet quark have two flavour states $q$ and $\tilde{q}$, which are related to each other by the unitary rotating $U_{\text{CKM}}$. Each generation of the left-handed $q$ and right-handed $u_{R}$ have the same flavour number, so their three generations individually conserve the global $U(1)_{u}\otimes U(1)_{c}\otimes U(1)_{t}$. Likewise, each generation of the left-handed $\tilde{q}$ and right-handed $d_{R}$ have the same flavour number, so their three generations individually conserve the global $U(1)_{d}\otimes U(1)_{s}\otimes U(1)_{b}$. The parallel explanations apply to the lepton case. Note that $\nu_{R\alpha}$ and $N_{L,R}$ are all the singlet fermions beyond the SM. $\nu_{R\alpha}$ is the light right-handed neutrino, but it is a dark fermion with the ``$-1$" dark parity under the dark symmetry $Z_{2}^{\text{Dark}}$, in contrast, $N_{L,R}$ is a superheavy neutral Dirac fermion without dark parity, it is actually a mediator between the SM leptons and the dark $\nu_{R}$, therefore it only conserves the total neutral lepton number $U(1)_{\nu_{1}+\nu_{2}+\nu_{3}}$. In brief, there are the six quark flavour states and the six lepton flavour states, furthermore, each of the fermion flavour states includes the left-handed and right-handed components, they individually conserve their corresponding flavour number.

 The model scalar particles and their vacuum states are given as follows,
\begin{alignat}{1}
 &H=\left[\begin{matrix}H^{+}\\H^{0}\end{matrix}\right],\hspace{0.3cm} \tilde{H}=i\tau_{2}H^{*},\hspace{0.3cm} 
   \Phi=\left[\begin{matrix}\Phi^{+}\\\Phi^{0}\end{matrix}\right],\hspace{0.3cm} \tilde{\Phi}=i\tau_{2}\Phi^{*},\hspace{0.3cm} 
   \phi=\phi^{*}, \nonumber\\
 &\langle H\rangle=\left[\begin{matrix}0\\\frac{v_{H}}{\sqrt{2}}\end{matrix}\right]\approx174\;\mathrm{GeV},\hspace{0.3cm} 
   \langle\Phi\rangle=\left[\begin{matrix}0\\\frac{v_{\Phi}}{\sqrt{2}}\end{matrix}\right]\sim10\;\mathrm{eV},\hspace{0.3cm} \langle\phi\rangle=v_{\phi}\sim10\;\mathrm{TeV}, \\
 &Z_{2}^{\text{Dark}}\xrightarrow{\langle\phi\rangle}\text{null}\,,\hspace{0.5cm}
   SU(2)_{L}\otimes U(1)_{Y}\xrightarrow{\langle H\rangle}U(1)_{\text{em}}\,, \nonumber
\end{alignat}
 where $\tau_{2}$ is the second Pauli matrix, the scalar potential and its vacuum spontaneous breaking were in detail discussed in \cite{1}. The doublet $H$ is namely the SM Higgs field, while both the doublet $\Phi$ and the real singlet $\phi$ are the dark scalar fields with the ``$-1$" dark parity under $Z_{2}^{\text{Dark}}$. $\Phi$ has inherently a superheavy mass about $10^{11}$ GeV, which also serves as the inflation field in \cite{1}. In Eq. (3), these three vacuum expectation values are explicitly hierarchical, so they also indicate the sequence of the dark and gauge symmetry breakings. After the above vacuum breakings, $H^{0}$ and $\phi$ will respectively become the SM Higgs boson (whose mass has been measured as $125$ GeV) and the dark scalar boson (whose mass is predicted as several TeV), but $\Phi$ still keeps its original structure due to $v_{\Phi}\ll M_{\Phi}$, of course, it only appears in very early universe and is absent at low-energy scale. Note that all of the fermion flavour symmetries are unaffected by the above breakings.

 Based on the above-mentioned particle contents and all kinds of the model symmetries, we can write out the relevant Lagrangian in what follows. The Yukawa interactions are
\begin{alignat}{1}
 \mathscr{L}_{\text{Yukawa}}=&-\overline{\tilde{q}}\left[\begin{matrix}m_{d}&&\\&m_{s}&\\&&m_{b}\end{matrix}\right]d_{R}\frac{H\sqrt{2}}{v_{H}}
   -\overline{q}\left[\begin{matrix}m_{u}&&\\&m_{c}&\\&&m_{t}\end{matrix}\right]u_{R}\frac{\tilde{H}\sqrt{2}}{v_{H}} \nonumber\\
 &-\overline{\tilde{l}}\left[\begin{matrix}m_{e}&&\\&m_{\nu}&\\&&m_{\tau}\end{matrix}\right]e_{R}\frac{H\sqrt{2}}{v_{H}}
   -\overline{l}\left[\begin{matrix}y_{1}&&\\&y_{2}&\\&&y_{3}\end{matrix}\right]\nu_{R}\tilde{\Phi} \nonumber\\
 &-\overline{l}\left[\begin{matrix}x_{1}\\x_{2}\\x_{3}\end{matrix}\right]N_{R}\tilde{H}-\overline{N_{L}}\,[x_{1},x_{2},x_{3}]\,\nu_{R}\phi
   -M_{N}\overline{N_{L}}N_{R}+h.c.\,,
\end{alignat}
where the quark masses and the charged lepton masses (and the $U_{\text{CKM}}$ elements in the following Eq. (5)) are the well-known flavour parameters in the SM, whereas $y_{1,2,3}$, $x_{1,2,3}$ and $M_{N}$ are namely the flavour parameters beyond the SM, note that $y_{1,2,3}\sim10^{-2}$ is the same size as the Yukawa couplings of the charged lepton, while $x_{1,2,3}\sim10^{-4}$ is assumed as a weak coupling. In Eq. (4), besides the gauge symmetry constraint, $\nu_{R}$ is only coupling to either $\tilde{\Phi}$ or $\phi$ due to the $Z_{2}^{\text{Dark}}$ constraint, in addition, the fermion flavour symmetries in Eq. (2) automatically guarantee the global conservations of both the baryon number and the lepton number, so any Majorana-type mass or couplings are all prohibited. In the first two lines of Eq. (4), all kinds of the flavour conservations in Eq. (2) naturally constrain that the Yukawa couplings of the quarks and leptons are all diagonal matrices, namely any non-diagonal element must be zero. In fact, only the diagonal elements in the Yukawa matrices make physical senses, which are related to the quark and lepton mass parameters, while those non-diagonal elements have no physical significance at all because they can not be measured by any particle experiments. In the last line of Eq. (4), the superheavy $N$ is respectively coupling to the lighter $l$ and $\nu_{R}$ with the equal coupling coefficients $x_{1,2,3}$, thus the individual $U(1)_{\nu_{1}}\otimes U(1)_{\nu_{2}}\otimes U(1)_{\nu_{3}}$ is explicitly violated but the total $U(1)_{\nu_{1}+\nu_{2}+\nu_{3}}$ is still conserved. $M_{N}\sim10^{8}$ GeV is the inherent mass of $N$, so $N$ can not appear at the low-energy scale. Finally, the quark masses, the charged lepton masses and the coupling coefficients $x_{1,2,3}$ are all real parameters since their complex phases can all be absorbed into the relevant fermion field redefinition. Only the coupling coefficients $y_{1,2,3}$ remain complex parameters because their phases are irremovable further, these phases thus become the only CP-violating source in the Yukawa sector, at the same time, they are also the only CP-violating source beyond the SM.

 In the gauge kinetic energy terms of the model Lagrangian, by use of Eq. (2), we can directly derive the charged current couplings of the quarks and that of the leptons, they are
\begin{alignat}{1}
 \mathscr{L}_{\text{Gauge}}\supset\, &-\frac{g}{\sqrt{2}}\,\overline{q}\,\gamma^{\mu}(W_{\mu}^{+}\tau^{+}+W_{\mu}^{-}\tau^{-})q
   =-\frac{g}{\sqrt{2}}\,\overline{\tilde{q}}\,\gamma^{\mu}(W_{\mu}^{+}\tau^{+}+W_{\mu}^{-}\tau^{-})\tilde{q} \nonumber\\
 &\hspace{0.5cm}=-\frac{g}{\sqrt{2}}\,(\overline{u_{L}}\gamma^{\mu}W_{\mu}^{+}U_{\text{CKM}}d_{L}
   +\overline{d_{L}}\gamma^{\mu}W_{\mu}^{-}U^{\dagger}_{\text{CKM}}u_{L})\,, \nonumber\\
 &-\frac{g}{\sqrt{2}}\,\overline{l}\,\gamma^{\mu}(W_{\mu}^{+}\tau^{+}+W_{\mu}^{-}\tau^{-})l
   =-\frac{g}{\sqrt{2}}\,\overline{\tilde{l}}\,\gamma^{\mu}(W_{\mu}^{+}\tau^{+}+W_{\mu}^{-}\tau^{-})\tilde{l} \nonumber\\
 &\hspace{0.5cm}=-\frac{g}{\sqrt{2}}\,(\overline{\nu_{L}}\gamma^{\mu}W_{\mu}^{+}U_{\text{CKM}}e_{L}
   +\overline{e_{L}}\gamma^{\mu}W_{\mu}^{-}U^{\dagger}_{\text{CKM}}\nu_{L})\,,
\end{alignat}
 where the notations are self-explanatory. It is seen from Eq. (4) that the flavour states of $u_{L\alpha}$, $d_{L\alpha}$ and $e_{L\alpha}$ are exactly their mass eigenstates but the flavour state $\nu_{L\alpha}$ is not its mass eigenstate yet. Obviously, all the relations in Eq. (5) automatically satisfy those flavour conservations in Eq. (2), at the same time, the neutral current couplings (which are not written out here) of course satisfy those flavour conservations as well, therefore the fermion flavour symmetries in Eq. (2) are actually universal for all of the gauge coupling terms. Note that $u_{L}$ and $d'_{L}=U_{\text{CKM}}d_{L}$ are the same flavour so that they conserve $U(1)_{u}\otimes U(1)_{c}\otimes U(1)_{t}$, while $d_{L}$ and $u'_{L}=U^{\dagger}_{\text{CKM}}u_{L}$ are the same flavour so that they conserve $U(1)_{d}\otimes U(1)_{s}\otimes U(1)_{b}$. In brief, each of the gauge coupling terms of the quarks can only conserve either $U(1)_{u}\otimes U(1)_{c}\otimes U(1)_{t}$ or $U(1)_{d}\otimes U(1)_{s}\otimes U(1)_{b}$, but it can not simultaneously conserve them both. The parallel discussions apply to the lepton gauge couplings. However, it should  be stressed that $U_{\text{CKM}}$ naturally appears in the charged current couplings of the quarks and leptons but rather disappears in the neutral current couplings, this is purely derived from the arrangements of the fermion flavour states and symmetries in Eq. (2), it has nothing to do with the Yukawa couplings in Eq. (4). Finally, note that i) the complex phase in $U_{\text{CKM}}$ is the only CP-violating source in the SM, ii) as yet $U_{\text{CKM}}$ in the charged current couplings of the leptons can not be identified as $U_{\text{PMNS}}$ because $\nu_{L\alpha}$ are not the neutrino mass eigenstates.

 The full scalar Lagrangian has been given and discussed in \cite{1}, here I only write out the relevant part for the purpose of this paper,
\begin{alignat}{1}
\mathscr{L}_{\text{Scalar}}\supset\,\mu_{0}(H^{\dagger}\Phi\,\phi+\phi\,\Phi^{\dagger}H)-M^{2}_{\Phi}\,\Phi^{\dagger}\Phi+\cdots,
\end{alignat}
 where $\mu_{0}\sim10^{8}$ GeV is the parameter of the triple scalar couplings. Since both $M_{N}$ and $\mu_{0}$ are at the energy scale of $\sim10^{8}$ GeV, then this indicates that they may result from a special symmetry breaking at this super-high scale. In the reference \cite {1}, $\Phi$ serves as the inflation field, I discuss its inflation evolution and obtain the inflationary potential and $M_{\Phi}\sim10^{11}$ GeV, at the end of the inflation the $\Phi$ decay can cause the reheating universe and the primordial plasma. In the current paper, these terms in Eq. (6) have important implications for both the neutrino mass generation and the leptogenesis.

 Since both $N$ and $\Phi$ are superheavy particles, which are absent at the low-energy scale, thus they can be integrated out from Eq. (4) and Eq. (6), then we obtain the effective neutrino couplings and the neutrino mass matrix, they are given as follows,
\begin{alignat}{1}
 &\mathscr{L}^{\text{eff}}_{\text{neutrino}}=-\overline{l}\left(\frac{\mu_{0}}{M_{\Phi}^{2}}\left[\begin{matrix}y_{1}&&\\&y_{2}&\\&&y_{3}\end{matrix}\right]
   -\frac{1}{M_{N}}\left[\begin{matrix}x^{2}_{1}&x_{1}x_{2}&x_{1}x_{3}\\x_{1}x_{2}&x^{2}_{2}&x_{2}x_{3}\\x_{1}x_{3}&x_{2}x_{3}&x^{2}_{3}
   \end{matrix}\right]\right)\nu_{R}\phi\tilde{H}+h.c. \nonumber\\
 &\hspace{1.5cm}=-\overline{l}\,M_{\nu}\,\nu_{R}\frac{\phi\tilde{H}\sqrt{2}}{v_{\phi}v_{H}}+h.c.\,,\hspace{0.5cm}M_{\nu}=M_{1}+M_{2}\,,\nonumber\\
 &M_{1}=\frac{\mu_{0}v_{\phi}v_{H}}{\sqrt{2}M^{2}_{\Phi}}\left[\begin{matrix}y_{1}&&\\&y_{2}&\\&&y_{3}\end{matrix}\right]
   =\frac{v_{\Phi}}{\sqrt{2}}\left[\begin{matrix}y_{1}&&\\&y_{2}&\\&&y_{3}\end{matrix}\right]
   =v_{1}\left[\begin{matrix}a_{1}e^{i\varphi}&&\\&a_{2}&\\&&1\end{matrix}\right], \nonumber\\
 &M_{2}=-\frac{v_{\phi}v_{H}}{\sqrt{2}M_{N}}
   \left[\begin{matrix}x^{2}_{1}&x_{1}x_{2}&x_{1}x_{3}\\x_{1}x_{2}&x^{2}_{2}&x_{2}x_{3}\\x_{1}x_{3}&x_{2}x_{3}&x^{2}_{3}\end{matrix}\right]
   =-v_{2}\left[\begin{matrix}b^{2}_{1}&b_{1}b_{2}&b_{1}\\b_{1}b_{2}&b^{2}_{2}&b_{2}\\b_{1}&b_{2}&1\end{matrix}\right],\\
  \text{with}\hspace{0.2cm}&\mu_{0}\sim M_{N}\sim10^{8}\;\text{GeV},\hspace{0.3cm} M_{\Phi}\sim10^{11}\;\text{GeV},\hspace{0.3cm}
  y_{3}\sim10^{-2},\hspace{0.3cm} x_{3}\sim10^{-4},
\end{alignat}
 where $v_{\Phi}=\frac{\mu_{0}v_{\phi}v_{H}}{M^{2}_{\Phi}}\sim10$ eV is namely the tiny vacuum expectation value of $\Phi$. When the effective neutrino couplings is compared with the Yukawa couplings of the quarks and charged leptons in Eq. (4), $M_{\nu}$ exceptionally consists of $M_{1}$ and $M_{2}$ (note that $M_{2}$ is negative), the $M_{1}$ term still keeps the individual neutrino flavour conservation, which is similar to the cases of the quarks and charged leptons, but the $M_{2}$ term explicitly violates it, it only conserves the total neutrino flavour number, therefore the $M_{2}$ term is the source of the neutrino flavour violation and flavour mixing. In Eq. (7), I employ seven independent effective parameters of $[v_{1},v_{2},\varphi,a_{1},a_{2},b_{1},b_{2}]$ to replace the original ones, in which $\varphi$ is the only remained complex phase. By use of Eq. (8) there are naturally $v_{1}\sim v_{2}\sim0.1$ eV and $[a_{1},a_{2},b_{1},b_{2}]\sim1$, thus $M_{\nu}$ is completely determined by these seven effective parameters, their values will be determined by fitting the neutrino mass spectrum and the $U_{\text{PMNS}}$ mixing angles. Note that $\varphi$ is now the only CP-violating source beyond the SM, it will play a key role in the following leptogenesis. Obviously, this mechanism of generating neutrino mass is a Dirac-type seesaw, which is very different from the traditional Majorana-type seesaw \cite{13}. 

 In Eq. (7), $M_{\nu}$ is obviously a complex symmetric matrix, so it can be parameterized by three mass eigenvalues $m_{1,2,3}$ and one unitary matrix $U_{\nu}$, simultaneously, the neutrino flavour states are related to its mass eigenstates by $U_{\nu}$, the detailed relations are as follows,
\begin{alignat}{1}
 &M_{\nu}=U_{\nu}\left[\begin{matrix}m_{1}e^{i\epsilon_{1}}&&\\&m_{2}e^{i\epsilon_{2}}&\\&&m_{3}e^{i\epsilon_{3}}\end{matrix}\right]U_{\nu}^{T},\hspace{0.3cm}
   \nu_{L}=U_{\nu}\,\nu_{L}^{\text{mass}},\hspace{0.3cm}
   \nu_{R}=U_{\nu}^{*}\left[\begin{matrix}e^{-i\epsilon_{1}}&&\\&e^{-i\epsilon_{2}}&\\&&e^{-i\epsilon_{3}}\end{matrix}\right]\nu_{R}^{\text{mass}},\nonumber\\
 &U_{\nu}=\left[\begin{matrix}e^{i\eta_{1}}&&\\&e^{i\eta_{2}}\approx1&\\&&1\end{matrix}\right]\left[\begin{matrix}
   c_{12}c_{13}&s_{12}c_{13}&s_{13}e^{-i\delta}\\-s_{_{12}}c_{23}-c_{12}s_{23}s_{13}e^{i\delta}&c_{_{12}}c_{23}-s_{12}s_{23}s_{13}e^{i\delta}&s_{23}c_{13}\\
   s_{_{12}}s_{23}-c_{12}c_{23}s_{13}e^{i\delta}&-c_{_{12}}s_{23}-s_{12}c_{23}s_{13}e^{i\delta}&c_{23}c_{13}\end{matrix}\right],
\end{alignat}
 where $U_{\nu}$ is given by the standard form, there are $s_{ij}=\sin\theta_{ij}$ and $c_{ij}=\cos\theta_{ij}$. In Eq. (9), these three phases of $\epsilon_{1,2,3}$ have no physical significance since they can be absorbed into the $\nu_{R}^{\text{mass}}$ redefinition, there are in total nine physically meaningful parameters, they are three mass eigenvalues, three mixing angles and three complex phases $[\delta,\eta_{1},\eta_{2}]$, nevertheless later the numerical calculations actually give $e^{i\eta_{2}}\approx1$.

 Now put $\nu_{L}=U_{\nu}\nu_{L}^{\text{mass}}$ into Eq. (5), namely the left-handed neutrino flavour states are replaced by its mass eigenstates, eventually we derive the lepton mixing matrix as
\ba
 U_{\text{PMNS}}=U^{\dagger}_{\text{CKM}}U_{\nu}\,.
\ea
 This equation clearly shows a relationship between the quark mixing and the lepton one, at the same time, it also indicates the source of the difference between them. If there were no the superheavy $N$ in Eq. (4), the neutrino flavour would always be conserved as same as the quark and charged lepton cases, then there is only the $M_{1}$ term in Eq. (7), while the $M_{2}$ term is vanishing, thus there is no the neutrino mixing, namely $U_{\nu}$ would be a unit matrix, eventually the lepton mixing would be as same as the quark mixing, namely shown in Eq. (5). By this point all of these things have been very clear and manifest.

 From the systems of equations of Eq. (7) and Eq. (9),  once we input a set of values of these parameters $[\varphi,a_{1},a_{2},b_{1},b_{2},v_{1},v_{2}]$, then we can immediately calculate out $m_{1,2,3}$ and $U_{\nu}$ by a computer program, and then put $U_{\nu}$ into Eq. (10) to test whether it holds since $U_{\text{CKM}}$, $U_{\text{PMNS}}$, $\triangle m^{2}_{21}$ and $\triangle m^{2}_{32}$ have been all fixed in Eq. (1), thus we can find out the numerical solutions of $M_{\nu}$ which are fitting to these experimental data. The detail calculated results are all listed in Table 1. 
