\vspace{0.6cm}
\noindent\textbf{III. Leptogenesis}

\vspace{0.3cm}
 In the paper of \cite{1}, the author discussed the leptogenesis mechanism through the CP-asymmetric decay of the inflation field $\Phi\rightarrow l^{c}+\nu_{R}$, which occurs in the reheating period, but this scenario is based on the premise that $\Gamma(\Phi\rightarrow l^{c}+\nu_{R})\ll\Gamma(\Phi\rightarrow H+\phi)$. In the light of Eqs. (4), (6) and (8), a simple calculation gives $\Gamma(\Phi\rightarrow l^{c}+\nu_{R})/\Gamma(\Phi\rightarrow H+\phi)=(y_{\alpha}y_{\alpha}^{*})/(\frac{\mu_{0}}{M_{\Phi}})^{2}\sim10^{2}$, so the original scenario is certainly invalid under the parameter selection of the current paper. Therefore I here suggest a new leptogenesis scenario, it naturally arises from the two CP-asymmetric decays of $N\rightarrow l+H$ and $N\rightarrow\nu_{R}+\phi$, which occur in the primordial radiation after the reheating.

 According to Eq. (4), the $N$ decay have only two channels of $N\rightarrow l+H$ and $N\rightarrow\nu_{R}+\phi$, moreover, their decay rates are of the same order of magnitude, Figure 1 draws their tree and one-loop diagrams.
\begin{figure}
 \centering
 \includegraphics[totalheight=6.5cm]{Fig-1.eps}
 \caption{The tree and one-loop diagrams of $N\rightarrow l+H$ and $N\rightarrow\nu_{R}+\phi$. The CP asymmetry of $N\rightarrow l+H$ can generate asymmetric lepton, while the CP asymmetry of $N\rightarrow\nu_{R}+\phi$ can generate asymmetric $\nu_{R}$, these two asymmetries are of the same amounts but opposite signs since the net total lepton number is conserved as zero. Below $T=M_{N}$, $\nu_{R}$ is decoupling from the primordial plasma, the $\nu_{R}$ asymmetry is forever frozen in the dark sector, whereas the lepton asymmetry is partly converted into the baryon asymmetry through the SM sphaleron transition.}
\end{figure}
 However, these two decays have the following three remarkable features.  i) These two decays are out-of-equilibrium because their decay rates are smaller than the universe expansion rate. ii) Each decay rate has its own $CP$ asymmetry due to the interference between the tree amplitude and the one-loop one, the CP-violating source is exactly the complex phase $\varphi$ in $M_{\nu}$, as a result, at the same time these two decays can respectively lead to the lepton asymmetry and the $\nu_{R}$ asymmetry, nevertheless they are of the same amounts but opposite signs since the net total lepton number is conserved as zero in the universe. iii) Below $T=M_{N}$, $\nu_{R}$ is actually decoupling from the primordial plasma, thus the generated lepton asymmetry and $\nu_{R}$ asymmetry are naturally separated from each other, they respectively inhabit in the SM sector and dark sector, thus they can never be erased each other. Since $\nu_{R}$ and its asymmetry are forever frozen in the dark sector, they are only diluted as the universe expansion, by contrast, in the SM sector the lepton asymmetry can partly be converted into the baryon asymmetry through the electroweak sphaleron transition \cite{14}. In short, this scenario is a Dirac-type leptogenesis \cite{15a}, although it does not fully fulfil the Sakharov's three conditions \cite{15b}, it can be indeed put into effect on generating the matter-antimatter asymmetry in the universe.

 The relevant calculations of the $N$ decay are given as follows,
\begin{alignat}{1}
 &\Gamma_{N}=\Gamma(N\rightarrow l+H)+\Gamma(N\rightarrow\nu_{R}+\phi),\hspace{0.3cm}
   \Gamma_{\overline{N}}=\Gamma(N^{c}\rightarrow l^{c}+H^{c})+\Gamma(N^{c}\rightarrow\nu_{R}^{c}+\phi), \nonumber\\
 &\Gamma_{N}=\Gamma_{\overline{N}}\approx\frac{3M_{N}}{32\pi}\sum\limits_{\alpha}x^{2}_{\alpha}
   =\frac{3\sqrt{2}M_{N}^{2}\text{Tr}[-M_{2}]}{32\pi\,v_{\phi}v_{H}}
   <H=\frac{1.66\sqrt{g_{*}}\,T^{2}}{M_{\text{Pl}}}|_{T=M_{N}}, \nonumber\\
 &A_{\text{CP}}=\frac{\Gamma(N\rightarrow l+H)-\Gamma(N^{c}\rightarrow l^{c}+H^{c})}{\Gamma_{N}}
   =-\frac{\Gamma(N\rightarrow\nu_{R}+\phi)-\Gamma(N^{c}\rightarrow\nu_{R}^{c}+\phi)}{\Gamma_{N}} \nonumber\\
 &\hspace{0.8cm}\approx-\frac{\frac{\mu_{0}}{M_{N}}\,\text{Im}\left[\sum\limits_{\alpha}x^{2}_{\alpha}y^{*}_{\alpha}\right]}
   {6\pi\sum\limits_{\alpha}x^{2}_{\alpha}}f(\frac{M^{2}_{N}}{M^{2}_{\Phi}})
   =-\frac{\sqrt{2}\frac{\mu_{0}}{M_{N}}\,\text{Im}\text{Tr}[M_{2}M_{1}^{*}]}{6\pi\,v_{\Phi}\text{Tr}[M_{2}]}
   f(\frac{M^{2}_{N}}{M^{2}_{\Phi}})\,, \\
 & f(\frac{M^{2}_{N}}{M^{2}_{\Phi}})=f(x)=1+\frac{\ln(1+x)}{x}=2-\frac{x}{2}+\frac{x^{2}}{3}-\frac{x^{3}}{4}+\cdots\approx2\,,\nonumber
\end{alignat}
 where by use of Eq. (7) $M_{1}$ and $M_{2}$ are respectively substitute for $y_{\alpha}$ and $x^{2}_{\alpha}$, thus we can clearly see that these relations are closely related to the neutrino mass and mixing. $M_{\text{Pl}}=1.22\times10^{19}$ GeV is the Planck mass, the effective number of relativistic degrees of freedom is $g_{*}=113$ at $T=M_{N}$. Since there are $\text{Tr}[-M_{2}]\sim0.1$ eV and $v_{\phi}v_{H}\sim10^{6}$ GeV, then there is naturally $\Gamma_{N}<H$, so the $N$ decay is indeed out-of-equilibrium. On the other hand, from the numerical results in Table 1 we can calculate out $\text{Im}\text{Tr}[M_{2}M_{1}^{*}]/\text{Tr}[M_{2}]\sim5\times10^{-6}$ eV (note that it is positive), since there are $\frac{\mu_{0}}{M_{N}}\sim1$ and $v_{\Phi}\sim10$ eV, then we naturally obtain $A_{\text{CP}}\sim-6\times10^{-8}$, this value and its negative sign together are very vital for the correct baryon asymmetry. In Eq. (11), the CP-asymmetry source only originates from the CP-violating phase $\varphi$ in $M_{1}$ (which is beyond the SM), it has nothing to do with the CP-violating phase of $U_{\text{CKM}}$ in the charged current couplings (which is in the SM), nevertheless the lepton mixing $U_{\text{PMNS}}$ is simultaneously related to both of them by Eq. (10). Finally, by a simple calculation we can prove that the dilute process of $l^{c}+\nu_{R}\rightarrow\phi+H$ via the t-channel $N$ mediation is always invalid at low-energy temperature because its reaction rate is severely out-of-equilibrium. 

 After the $N$ decays are completed, as a result, these two CP asymmetries in Eq. (11) can respectively generate asymmetric lepton and asymmetric $\nu_{R}$ with the same amounts but opposite signs, although the net total lepton number is always conserved as zero. On the other hand, below $T=M_{N}$ the dark $\nu_{R}$ is actually decoupling from the primordial plasma, thus both $\nu_{R}$ and its asymmetry are forever frozen in the dark sector, they are only diluted as the universe expanding. From the entropy conservation, we can derive that in later period the $\nu_{R}$ effective temperature is $T^{3}_{\nu_{R}}/T^{3}\approx0.0363$ (whereas the $\nu_{L}$ effective temperature is $T^{3}_{\nu_{L}}/T^{3}=\frac{4}{11}$). In the SM sector, the lepton asymmetry is partly converted into the baryon asymmetry through the electroweak sphaleron transition which conserves the $B-L$ number. Therefore the present-day baryon asymmetry is simply calculated as follows \cite{16},
\begin{alignat}{1}
 &\eta_{B}=Y_{B}(T_{0})[\frac{s}{n_{\gamma}}]_{T_{0}}=Y_{B}(T_{\text{ew}})[\frac{s}{n_{\gamma}}]_{T_{0}}
   =c_{s}Y_{B-L}(T_{\text{ew}})[\frac{s}{n_{\gamma}}]_{T_{0}}=c_{s}Y_{B-L}(M_{N})[\frac{s}{n_{\gamma}}]_{T_{0}}\,, \nonumber\\
 &\text{at} \:T=M_{N},\hspace{0.2cm} Y_{B-L}=-\frac{n_{l}-\overline{n}_{l}}{s}=\frac{n_{\nu_{R}}-\overline{n}_{\nu_{R}}}{s}
  =-\frac{n_{N}A_{\text{CP}}}{s}\approx-0.42\,\frac{A_{\text{CP}}}{g_{*}}\,, 
\end{alignat}
 where $T_{0}$ denotes the present-day universe temperature, $Y_{B}(T)=[\frac{n_{B}-\overline{n}_{B}}{s}]_{T}$ is the yield, $s$ is the entropy density, $c_{s}=\frac{28}{79}$ is the SM sphaleron coefficient, and $[\frac{s}{n_{\gamma}}]_{T_{0}}\approx7.38$ is due to $g_{*}(T_{0})\approx4.1$ which includes the $\nu_{R}$ contribution. If Eq. (11) gives the precise value of $A_{\text{CP}}\approx-6.32\times10^{-8}$, then put it into Eq. (12), thus we can accurately predict $\eta_{B}\approx6.14\times10^{-10}$, which is exactly fitting to the observed value from the current multiple measurements \cite{17}. By the way, the number density $n_{N}$ is suppressed exponentially as the temperature is falling, so the main bulk of $Y_{B-L}$ is generated around $T=M_{N}$. If one uses Boltzmann equations to solve numerically $Y_{B-L}$, then the obtained result is actually the same order of magnitude as the one given by Eq. (12), but only the prefactor coefficient is slightly different, this difference is not significant because we can always compensate it by another choice of the $A_{\text{CP}}$ value.

 Finally, I would point out that there is $[n_{\nu_{R}}+\overline{n}_{\nu_{R}}]/n_{\gamma}=\frac{3}{4}\times0.0363\approx0.0272$ at the present day, whereas the ratio of $[n_{\nu_{R}}-\overline{n}_{\nu_{R}}]/[n_{\nu_{R}}+\overline{n}_{\nu_{R}}]\approx-A_{\text{CP}}$ is never changed, therefore $[n_{\nu_{R}}-\overline{n}_{\nu_{R}}]/n_{\gamma}$ is actually the same order of magnitude as $\eta_{B}$, so the $\nu_{R}$ asymmetry can surely be ignored in the current universe. In addition, we can further calculate out the effective number of the neutrinos at the recombination as $N_{\text{eff}}=3[1+(T_{\nu_{R}}/T_{\nu_{L}})^{4}]\approx3.14$, which is also safely within the current limit given by the CMB data analysis \cite{18}. In a word, all of the results of the model are self-consistent and very well in agreement with the present measurements.

\vspace{0.6cm}
\noindent\textbf{IV. Conclusions}

\vspace{0.3cm}
  Based on the new extended model of the SM suggested in \cite{1}, here I specially discuss the flavour issues of the fermion mass and mixing in the model. First of all, I specifically set the fermion flavour states and their corresponding flavour symmetries, then I in detail discuss all kinds of the fermion flavour conservations in the Yukawa couplings as well as the gauge couplings, nevertheless the neutrino flavour conservation is violated by the superheavy neutral fermion $N$ couplings to the lighter left-handed $l$ or right-handed $\nu_{R}$, this not only directly leads to the neutrino mixing, and eventually make the difference between the quark mixing and the lepton mixing, but also it plays a key role in the leptogenesis. I derive the effective neutrino couplings and obtain the neutrino mass matrix, which has a special structure form and is described by seven effective parameters. The numerical solutions of $M_{\nu}$ are precisely fitting to all the data of the neutrino mass spectrum and lepton mixing $U_{\text{PMNS}}$, and clearly show the stable and stronger predicting power. In addition, I suggest the new scenario of the leptogenesis, which arises from the two CP-asymmetric decays in Figure 1, these two decays can respectively lead to the lepton asymmetry and the $\nu_{R}$ asymmetry with the same amounts but opposite signs, but these two asymmetries are shortly separated from each other as $\nu_{R}$ is decoupling below $T=M_{N}$, the former is partly converted into the baryon asymmetry through the SM sphaleron transition, whereas the latter is forever frozen in the dark sector. This leptogenesis scenario is closely related to the neutrino flavour violation, especially Eq. (11) gives its relationship with the neutrino mass and mixing, therefore it can simply and elegantly account for the present observed matter-antimatter asymmetry.

  In summary, this paper supplements a detail discussion about the fermion flavour issues for the model of \cite{1}, thus it becomes a more complete theory. This new theory covers both the SM physics (visible sector) and the BSM one (dark sector), it can successfully and excellently account for the fermion flavour issues, the matter-antimatter asymmetry, and some cosmological phenomena (the details are seen in \cite{1}). In particular, the realistic flavour symmetry suggested by this paper is both simple and believable, moreover, it is truly measurable by the particle experiments rather than an imaginary symmetry. We believe that the model is scientific, reasonable and realistic significance, therefore it is very worth studying in depth, and we also expect the ongoing and future experiments to test it.

