\subsection{Generic polynomials and numerics} 
\label{subsec generic} 
The generic polynomial for a given 
Herbrand invariant has the form $\left( \pi \sum_\sigma f_\sigma \pi^j x^i \right) + x^e$.  
Here $\sigma$ runs over all nonnegative integers for which $P_\sigma$ is drawn,
but not negligible.  To make structure more evident, we replace $f_\sigma$ by 
$d_\sigma$, $a_\sigma$, $b_\sigma$, or $c_\sigma$ according to whether
$P_\sigma$ is a $D$-point, $A$-point, $B$-point, or $C$-point.    So when these coefficients
run independently over $\tilde{\kappa}$, except for the inequalities 
$d_0, a_\sigma \neq 0$, one
gets the set $\EisenII(I/K)$.    As an example beyond \eqref{eq nonicgenpoly}, the generic 
polynomial corresponding to the $2$-adic Herbrand invariant $[1,2,2.5] = (1,3,5) = \langle 0.5,1.25,1.875 \rangle$ 
of Figures~\ref{fig slopepolygon}, \ref{fig herbrandphi} and \ref{fig examp8} is 
 \begin{eqnarray}
\nonumber \lefteqn{f(a_4,a_{10},a_{15},b_{14},b_{17},b_{19},c_8,c_{16},c_{20}; \pi;  x)  =  (\pi + \pi^2 c_8 + \pi^3 c_{16}) +  \pi^3 b_{17} x + } \\
\label{eq octicgenpoly}&&  
   \pi^2 a_{10} x^2 + \pi^3 b_{19} x^3+   (\pi a_4 + \pi^3 c_{20}) x^4 +
  \pi^2 b_{14} x^6+
 \pi^2 a_{15} x^7+ 
 x^8.
 \end{eqnarray}
 Other examples are given in the next sections.

