Computer vision algorithms have used epipolar geometry for decades \cite{lerman1970computer}.
Epipolar geometry for optical sensors constrains the location where a feature in one image might be found in another given a known relative pose between the sensors. 
For many computer vision algorithms, including the recovery of depth information \cite{rajagopalan2004depth}, epipolar geometry is an essential component.
Our work is related to epipolar geometry in that we use the same geometric concepts to determine how a feature visible in one sonar image projects to the image space of a second sonar given a known relative pose between the sensors. 

